# Human comprehension gate (global)

In the **Cursor IDE** this rule applies in every project once the kit is installed —
no per-project install needed. The **Cursor CLI** (`agent` binary) does not read
`~/.cursor/rules/`; run `./scripts/kit install --target=cursor --project` per repo so
the gate applies there too.

The developer is the **control plane**. Highly reliable automation reduces
monitoring; this gate counters that with an explicit human checkpoint before commit.

## Before commit or PR (non-trivial work)

1. Write `.ai/work/{work_ref}-handoff.md` in the **current project repo**
   (create `.ai/work/` if missing). Cover: what changed, data flow, key files.
2. Generate **3 comprehension questions** from the handoff — the human answers in
   their own words.
3. Wait for explicit **human sign-off**. Never fill in the sign-off or the answers
   on the human's behalf.
4. Only then run verification or commit.

**Default tier:** standard. Skip only when the human explicitly says comprehension is
minimal (typo or comment-only change). Strict tier adds a teach-back and 5 questions.

Full rules: `docs/guidelines/COMPREHENSION.md` in the kit tree (`kit_root` in
`~/.cursor/kit-user-rules.manifest.json`), also listed in that manifest under
`always_load_kit_guidelines_resolved`.
